// The half of the rule that has no range to compare against. `usize`'s width belongs to the TARGET, not
// to this compiler — kama accepts arbitrary triples — so `primIntRange` refuses to assert a range for it,
// and always has. But a NEGATIVE constant is out of range on every possible target, and that answer needs
// no width at all. Same for `uint64`. This is the whole of what the sign test buys, and it is the one
// rejection here that a real program is likely to have written by accident.
fn int32 main() {
    usize n = -1;
    return cast<int32>(n);
}
